  "notes": "West leads the ♥J against 3NT. South has 7 top tricks (2 spades, 3 hearts, and 2 clubs). After winning the heart opening lead, South should try to get 2 additional tricks from diamonds.\n\nIf South starts by playing a diamond to the ♦K or ♦Q, East will duck holding 2 or 3 diamonds to the ♦A. As dummy has only 1 entry, South will be unable to come to a second diamond trick and the contract will be in trouble.\n\nSouth should duck the first round of diamonds in the dummy. The defense will win cheaply, but the contract will be secure as long as diamonds are 3-2.\n\nSouth must be careful to win a club return in the closed hand, preserving the ♣K for a late dummy entry. A diamond is now played to the ♦K or ♦Q. South will not only make his contract (when diamonds are 3-2), but 2 overtricks as well. The recommended line of play will also succeed if either defender was dealt a singleton ♦A.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♥J against 3NT. South has 7 top tricks (2 spades, 3 hearts, and 2 clubs). After winning the heart opening lead, South should try to get 2 additional tricks from diamonds.",
